WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.533
Several hours had passed since the train had left Fix and Aouda at Fort Kearney station.

2
00:00:15.534 --> 00:00:20.426
The weather was dismal. Fix sat motionless on a bench in the station.

3
00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:28.829
Aouda, despite the bitter cold, kept stepping outside, walking to the end of the platform and peering through the heavily falling snow.

4
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.110
She would return chilled and remain inside only long enough to warm up.

5
00:00:33.111 --> 00:00:37.174
Then she would exit again, looking for any sign of the rescue party.

6
00:00:37.175 --> 00:00:41.273
The commander of the fort waited with them, careful to hide his worry.

7
00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:45.657
Night fell, but the little band of rescuers still did not return.

8
00:00:45.658 --> 00:00:49.879
As the falling snow diminished, the air became intensely cold.

9
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.204
Where could they be? Had they found the Indians?

10
00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:58.849
Were they fighting with them, or were they still wandering in the cold? Aouda suffered through the night.

11
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:04.675
Her heart was filled with anguish, and her imagination was full of the dangers Mr. Fogg faced.

12
00:01:04.676 --> 00:01:08.050
At dawn the sun rose above a misty horizon.

13
00:01:08.051 --> 00:01:13.573
It was now possible to see objects two miles off, but the horizon was still vacant.

14
00:01:13.574 --> 00:01:18.975
At seven o'clock the captain betrayed his worry and began to rub his hands anxiously.

15
00:01:18.976 --> 00:01:22.788
Then gunshots were heard. Was it a signal?

16
00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:26.103
The soldiers who had remained in the fort rushed out.

17
00:01:26.104 --> 00:01:29.875
Half a mile to the south, they could see a small group approaching.

18
00:01:29.876 --> 00:01:36.875
Mr. Fogg was riding at the head of the squad, and just behind him were Passepartout and the other two missing passengers.

19
00:01:36.876 --> 00:01:42.001
They had met and fought the Indians ten miles south of Fort Kearney.

20
00:01:42.002 --> 00:01:50.237
Passepartout and his companions had managed to escape their ropes and were already struggling against the Indians when Mr. Fogg and the soldiers arrived.

21
00:01:50.238 --> 00:01:53.098
The fight had been quick and successful.

22
00:01:53.099 --> 00:01:57.274
They were all welcomed back at the station with a joyful cheer.

23
00:01:57.275 --> 00:02:01.458
Phileas Fogg distributed the reward he had promised to the soldiers.

24
00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:06.537
Aouda took her protector's hand and pressed it to her own, too moved even to speak.

25
00:02:06.538 --> 00:02:09.847
Meanwhile Passepartout was looking around for the train.

26
00:02:09.848 --> 00:02:13.260
He thought he would find it there, ready to start for Omaha.

27
00:02:13.261 --> 00:02:16.907
He hoped that they could make up the lost time.

28
00:02:16.908 --> 00:02:20.162
"The train! The train!" cried Passepartout.

29
00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:22.270
"Gone," said Fix.

30
00:02:22.271 --> 00:02:26.075
"And when does the next train pass here?" asked Mr. Fogg.

31
00:02:26.076 --> 00:02:29.257
"Not until this evening," explained Aouda.

32
00:02:29.258 --> 00:02:33.418
"Ah," said Mr. Fogg quietly.

33
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:39.638
They were now twenty hours behind schedule. Fix approached Mr. Fogg with a possible solution.

34
00:02:39.639 --> 00:02:46.238
While Fix had been sitting in the station, a man had approached him and offered him a ride on a sledge with sails.

35
00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:50.958
Fix pointed to the man, who was walking up and down in front of the station.

36
00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:56.183
Mr. Fogg paused. He had never heard of any vehicle like the one described.

37
00:02:56.184 --> 00:03:02.900
He went up to the man, and after a brief exchange, the two entered a hut a short distance from the fort.

38
00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:09.942
Mr. Fogg examined the curious device. A low frame was mounted on two long runners, like skis.

39
00:03:09.943 --> 00:03:15.807
A high mast was attached to the frame, held firmly by thin metal wires.

40
00:03:15.808 --> 00:03:18.796
Attached to the mast was a wide sail.

41
00:03:18.797 --> 00:03:27.816
The owner of the vehicle, a man named Mudge, said the sledge could take them to Omaha where they could catch a train to Chicago and finally to New York.

42
00:03:27.817 --> 00:03:30.730
Mr. Fogg made a bargain with Mudge.

43
00:03:30.731 --> 00:03:34.603
At eight o'clock the sledge was ready.

44
00:03:34.604 --> 00:03:39.227
Mr. Fogg, Aouda, Passepartout, and Fix, took their places.

45
00:03:39.228 --> 00:03:46.206
The great sail was hoisted, and soon they were skimming across the frozen prairie at forty miles an hour.

46
00:03:46.207 --> 00:03:50.114
The travelers huddled close together to protect themselves from the cold.

47
00:03:50.115 --> 00:03:54.506
The freezing wind burned Passepartout's face as red as the setting sun.

48
00:03:54.507 --> 00:04:02.153
Aouda was cozily wrapped in fur. Mr. Fogg was as calm and confident as usual, sitting close beside Aouda.

49
00:04:02.154 --> 00:04:06.738
Fix was happy to be moving closer to England with Mr. Fogg in his sight.

50
00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:10.538
Passepartout knew that he would never forget Mr. Fogg's bravery.

51
00:04:10.539 --> 00:04:15.267
Nor would he forget the sacrifice that Mr. Fogg had made to rescue him from the Indians.

52
00:04:15.268 --> 00:04:19.582
Without hesitation, Fogg had risked his fortune and his life!

53
00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:22.674
Mudge steered his strange vehicle skillfully.

54
00:04:22.675 --> 00:04:28.971
Traveling on skis, they were able to cross the frozen Platte River and shorten the distance they would have traveled by train.

55
00:04:28.972 --> 00:04:33.909
They arrived at the station in Omaha just in time to catch the next train to Chicago.

56
00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:43.986
Mr. Fogg generously rewarded Mudge, and they jumped aboard the train as it slowly pulled out of the station.

57
00:04:43.987 --> 00:04:50.080
What a strange way to travel! I've seen a sledge before, but I've never seen a sledge with sails!

58
00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:54.076
A sledge is a vehicle that slides along grass or snow.

59
00:04:54.077 --> 00:04:57.948
It does not have wheels, but instead has runners, which are like skis.

60
00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:02.657
A sledge can carry heavy things such as wood, stone, or blocks of ice.

61
00:05:02.658 --> 00:05:08.838
Many American farmers have sledges, which can be moved by people, animals, or even sails on a windy day!

62
00:05:08.839 --> 00:05:13.905
A sledge is similar to a sled or a sleigh. Have you ridden a sled in winter?

63
00:05:13.906 --> 00:05:21.895
It's fun, isn't it? A sled is smaller than a sledge or a sleigh and is usually used for fun or to carry a light load.

64
00:05:21.896 --> 00:05:25.365
Animals, like horses or dogs, or people can pull sleds.

65
00:05:25.366 --> 00:05:28.700
A sleigh is much larger and is usually pulled by horses.

66
00:05:28.701 --> 00:05:39.465
What a fabulous adventure! I never expected to find myself sailing—and then skiing—across the American prairies!
